  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 223 AM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows strong high pressure in \nplace across NY and PA. Light SE flow was in place across Indiana, \nunder the influence of this high. Low pressure was found across SD, \nand thunderstorms were found ahead of a cold front over the high \nplains. Aloft, Ridging was found over the Central Plains states, \nwhile a trough was found along the Appalachians. This was resulting \nin NW flow across Indiana, and subsidence was shown exiting the \nstate. Some middle level moisture was shown arriving within the \nWabash valley. \n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nModels suggest the ridging over the plains will advance eastward, \nreaching the Great Lakes late tonight. NW flow is expected to remain \nin place today before shifting to the WNW tonight. Time heights and \nforecast soundings continue to show plentiful dry air within the \nlower and mid levels today and tonight. Models suggest that within \nthe lower levels our weather continues to be controlled by the \nstrong high pressure system to the east, with SE lower level flow \ntoday becoming light and southerly tonight. Thus mostly sunny skies \nwill once again be expected today and mostly clear skies tonight. \nLook for highs at or above persistence.\n\nSunday and Monday...\n\nRidging aloft over the Great Lakes on Sunday will shift to the \neastern Great Lakes and strengthen through Monday. This will shift \nthe steering flow aloft to westerly and allow any forcing dynamics \nto push well north of Indiana. Strong upper level high pressure \nlooks to set up over the southern plains and the Tennessee Valley.\nWithin the lower levels strong high pressure settles east of the \nCarolinas, allowing warm and humid gulf flow to develop. Strong warm \nair advection will be in place on Sunday and Monday, leading to the \narrival of warmer and more humid air as 850mb temps begin to surge \ntoward 20C. This will result in highs into the 90s and heat as  we \nend August, with mostly sunny days and mostly clear nights.\n\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...\n\nDry weather with continued heat is expected for the middle of next \nweek. Strong upper level high pressure in place over the Ohio and TN \nRiver valleys will keep all forcing dynamics steered well north to \nthe Great Lakes. Meanwhile within the lower levels, Indiana will be \nunder the influence of high pressure centered off the Carolina \ncoast. This will continue to allow hot and humid southerly flow to \nstream across Indiana. Forecast soundings on Tuesday suggest \nunreachable convective temperatures. On Wednesday, forecast \nsoundings suggest slightly lower but still very high convective \ntemperatures, with ample CAPE aloft. A diurnal shower or storm seems \npossible at this point, given the weaker cap. Highs in the middle \n90s will be expected.\n\nThursday and Friday...\n\nModels suggest strong ridging building aloft over the plains states. \nThis will result in NW upper flow across Indiana and the Ohio \nvalley. Some ridge riding forcing dynamics are suggested to spill \nacross Indiana on Thursday night into Friday. Meanwhile within the \nlower levels, a frontal boundary that was previously across the \nGreat Lakes is suggested to sag southward to Indiana and Ohio. \nForecast soundings still show strong capping on Thursday, but that \nis suggested to weaken on Friday as possible forcing dynamics pass \nwith very high pwats again. Thus at this point, chances for showers \nand storms on Thursday night and Friday are not out of the question. \nAgain, look for very warm highs in the 90s.",
